  "title": {
    "segmentId": "m00259:title:auto-1",
    "text": "Alpha Halogenation of Aldehydes and Ketones"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "c0b2123c-0cc3-4890-aeea-5e13fc3dd8a1"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00259:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00001",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00259:alt:fig-00001-alt",
          "text": "Acetophenone reacts with bromine in acetic acid to form alpha-bromoacetophenone in 72 percent yield."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_22_03_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00002",
      "segmentId": "m00259:para:para-00002"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00002",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00259:alt:fig-00002-alt",
          "text": "The figure shows three structures, dibromo acetaldehyde, bromoacetone, and 1,1,1 tribromoacetone. These are labeled as from the Hawaiian alga Asparagopsis taxiformis."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_22_03_002.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00003",
      "segmentId": "m00259:para:para-00003"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00003",
      "class": "mechanism-figure scaled-down",
      "caption": {
        "segmentId": "m00259:caption:fig-00003-caption"
      },
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00259:alt:fig-00003-alt",
          "text": "Four-step reaction mechanism for formation of bromoacetone from acetone and bromine with acid catalyst: protonation of carbonyl, abstraction of alpha proton to enol, addition of bromine on alpha carbon, deprotonation."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_22_03_003.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00004",
      "segmentId": "m00259:para:para-00004"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "id": "eq-00001",
      "class": "unnumbered"
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00006",
      "segmentId": "m00259:para:para-00006"
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00007",
      "segmentId": "m00259:para:para-00007"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00004",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00259:alt:fig-00004-alt",
          "text": "Ketone or aldehyde reacts slowly with hydronium ions to form enol intermediate, which reacts fast with trideuteriooxidanium ion and X 2 to form the product with D and X, respectively."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_22_03_004.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00008",
      "segmentId": "m00259:para:para-00008"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00005",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00259:alt:fig-00005-alt",
          "text": "2-methyl cyclohexanone reacts with bromine in acetic acid to form 2-bromo-2-methyl-cyclohexanone, which when heated reacts with pyridine to form 2-methyl-2-cyclohexenone in 63 percent yield."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_22_03_005.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
